Done in v2 [1]. The rules now have their own "rights" section, placed
right after "the list" and before everything else. It covers posting
too, and it says the README states the rules first. It also says
plainly that the rules rest on Dovecot's ACL and not on file
permissions, so a mistake in the ACL config would let every login
change the list. Acceptance steps 7 and 8 check the rules.

> Also is there any precedent for using IMAP folders in this way?

Yes. The pattern is old, though not common today. These are from
memory, and I have not checked them against current docs:

- The IMAP ACL extension, RFC 4314, defines the identifier "anyone"
  for exactly this. A folder can grant lookup and read to anyone,
  and write, insert, and expunge to named users. Dovecot's ACL plugin
  implements it.
- Cyrus IMAP at CMU served "bboards": shared folders for netnews and
  mailing lists, readable by anyone and written by a delivery agent.
  That is our design with a different server.
- public-inbox, the software behind lore.kernel.org, serves list
  archives over read-only IMAP with public-inbox-imapd. As I recall,
  it takes any login. That is the closest modern precedent. It is a
  read-only archive, though. Nobody archives or deletes through it.

What we add is two logins that may write: Pat moderating, and mbsync.
I know of no list that gives a moderator IMAP write access like this.
The ACL can express it, but it is the part with the least precedent.

> > +- notmuch indexes the mail root. Its pre-new hook runs mbsync first,
> > +  so one =notmuch new= syncs and indexes, and a failed sync stops it
>
> hrm... I don't think I want a network issue to stop notmuch from
> indexing. What's the point of this? Is it to not have a stale cache?

Yes, that was the point: one command to get current, so the agent
would never index a stale copy. The cost is the one you name. An
unreachable lab-mail stops indexing of mail already on disk.

> Let's talk through this a bit. I'm not sure this sync needs to happen at
> all. I'm fine with notmuch new just indexing what's new in the dir, not
> triggering a fetch.
>
> If I want a fetch, I can do it a different way.

Agreed, and v2 does it that way. notmuch has no pre-new hook. "notmuch
new" indexes only what is in the mail root and never contacts
lab-mail. A sync is its own command, run when wanted. A new acceptance
step checks that "notmuch new" succeeds with Dovecot stopped. Getting
the agent current before a pass is lab-process-mail's job, and the
spec leaves that out of scope.

> > +- msmtp posts through 127.0.0.1:2525 with no auth and no TLS, from
[...]
> Why no auth? I guess IMAP needs auth because users have different
> permissions, whereas with SMTP everyone has the same permission to
> deliver?

Exactly. Over IMAP the logins differ: two may change the list and the
rest may only read, so the two need secrets. Over SMTP every poster
has the same right, to add one message. A password would tell no two
posters apart, and every host would hold a secret that guards nothing.
The boundary for posting is the loopback bind. v2 states this reason
in the alternatives.

> > +- On beastie, notmuch-emacs reads the mail root and sends through
> > +  msmtp. A sent message is also saved by Fcc into
[...]
> What's the behavior of this once it syncs? Will there be duplicate
> message? Or they have the same ID, so it just gets a different tag?

No duplicate. notmuch keys a message by its Message-ID, so the Fcc
copy and the list's copy become one message with two files. Tags
belong to the message, not to a file, so the second file adds no tag.
The thread shows the message once, with "(2)" for two files in the
search view. I checked this with notmuch 0.40: I indexed a message in
sent/, added the same file in lists/, and ran notmuch new again. The
count stayed at 1, and --output=files listed both paths. v2 says this,
and acceptance step 3 now checks it.

